FORMAT: 1A
HOST: https://www.nitrous.io/api/v0/

# Nitrous API (Draft, 2016-03-01)

The Nitrous API exposes a set of endpoints that enable your application to
create and control workstations and projects for users of your application.

## How to get access and pricing

Contact us at hello@nitrous.io and we’ll setup a call to discuss the details of
your organization’s requirements.

## Terminology

* **Project**: A project is the most common unit that users interact with. Users
  connect to their project to edit code, run servers, and preview their
  application. Each project runs in its own isolated environment, distinct from
  other projects.
* **Workstation**: A workstation is a dedicated server allocated with CPU, memory,
  and disk storage resources. Projects are run on workstations and share the
  resources of the workstation.
* **Snapshot**: A snapshot is the state of a project at the time the snapshot was
  created. It is effectively a backup of a project, and can be used to create
  new projects. This is particularly useful for sharing a common project
  environment with multiple users.

## Authorization Guide

When your application has been accepted into our programme, you will be provided
with an OAuth 2.0 client ID and secret for use in your application. This allows
you to make requests to the Nitrous API on behalf of a user without accessing
passwords and other sensitive information.

OAuth 2.0 is a complex [specification](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6749), so
if you are unfamiliar with it, check out this
["OAuth 2 Simplified" article](https://aaronparecki.com/2012/07/29/2/oauth2-simplified)
as a starting point.

### Get authorization from the user [GET /oauth/authorize]

The first step is getting authorization from a user to let your application act
on their behalf. The user will be shown an HTML web form where they will be
authenticated with Nitrous, and is prompted to approve or deny your request.

After the Nitrous user authenticates and authorizes your application (by
submitting the HTML web form), Nitrous will issue an HTTP 302 Redirect to the
`redirect_uri`. The redirect URI will include an authorization code, e.g.

        https://your-redirect-uri/?code=AUTHORIZATION_CODE

+ Attributes
    + response_type (required, string) - OAuth 2.0 response type. `code` is the only acceptable value at this time
    + client_id (required, string) - The client ID of your application.
    + state (optional, string) - This will be returned to your application to prevent cross-site request forgery.
    + `redirect_uri` (optional, string) - The URI we will redirect the user to after they authorize your application. The base of the URI must match the `redirect_uri` of your application.

+ Response 200 (text/html)

### Exchange authorization token for access token [POST /oauth/token]

With the authorization code from the authorization request (i.e. the previous
step), exchange it for an access token. The access token will allow you to make
requests on behalf of the user.

Pass the `access_token` from the previous step in the `Authorization` header
with the type `Bearer` to make requests to other API endpoints on behalf of the
user, e.g.

        curl -H 'Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN' https://www.nitrous.io/api/v0/hosts

+ Attributes
    + `grant_type` (required, string) - `authorization_code` is the only acceptable value.
    + code (required, string) - The authorization code from the authorization request.
    + client_id (required, string) - The client ID of your application.
    + client_secret (required, string) - The client secret of your application.
    + `redirect_uri` (optional, string) - The `redirect_uri` used in the authorization request, if any.

+ Response 200 (application/json)
    + Attributes
        + access_token (string) - Access token that you allows your application to make requests on behalf of the user.
        + token_type (string) - The token will be a `Bearer` token.
        + scope (string) - Scope of the access token.
        + expires_in (number) - Number of seconds before this token expires.
    + Body

            {
                "access_token": "siZ-tVinPuxITv40DQ",
                "token_type": "Bearer",
                "scope": "third_party",
                "expires_in": 604800
            }


## Group Users

### Create a user [POST /users]

Your application can create a Nitrous user account for your users. This is
useful when you want to offer a seamless experience in your application without
requiring your users to signup for a Nitrous account first.

You will probably want to first create an organization, and then add any users
you create to your organization. Nitrous will assist you in creating the
organization and you will be given an org slug (this is your `org_slug`).

+ Request
    + Attributes
        + email (required, string) - Email address of the user.
        + username (required, string) - Username of the user. This must be unique.
        + password (required, string) - Password for the user.
        + name (optional, string) - Name of the user.
        + org_slug (optional, string) - The created user will be added as a member of the org with this slug.
        + `org_permission` (optional, string) - The role of the user in the org, if `org_slug` is specified. `member` and `limited` are the allowed values. A `limited` member will be restricted from seeing who are the other members of the organization.
        + remote_addr (required, string) - IP address of user.
        + client_id (required, string) - The client ID of your application.
        + client_secret (required, string) - The client secret of your application.

+ Response 201 (application/json)
    + Body

            {
                "user": {
                    "id": 42,
                    "name": "Mortimer Smith",
                    "username": "morty",
                    "email": "morty@wubbalubbadubdub.org",
                    "created_at": "2016-02-29T09:35:57.019617Z",
                    "updated_at": "2016-02-29T09:35:57.019617Z"
                }
            }


### Logging in users to Nitrous automatically [GET /user/login_token]

Sometimes, your application will need to login automatically for users. For
example, you might want to add a "Open in Nitrous" button somewhere in your
application (without requiring the user to login).

This endpoint will return a login token that can be used to login the user,
then redirect to a URL (on www.nitrous.io) of your choice. This token expires
in 1 minute.

For example, with a `login_token` of `badcafe`, you can redirect the user to
the Nitrous IDE of their project (slug `grandeur-firefly-42`) by sending
the user to this URL:

        https://www.nitrous.io/token_login?token=badcafe&redirect_to=%2Fide%2F%23%2Fgrandeur-firefly-2

+ Request

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer OAUTH2_ACCESS_TOKEN

+ Response 200 (application/json)
    + Body

            {
                "login_token": "eyJ0eXBlIjoibG9naW50b2tlbiIsInVzZXJfaWQiOjE"
            }


## Group Regions

A region is a geographical location in which workstations can be located. You
should create workstations in a region close to where your users are located so
that they will experience lower latencies connecting to their
workstations/projects.

### List All Regions [GET /regions]

+ Response 200 (application/json)
    + Attributes
        + id (number) - ID of region.
        + slug (string) - A unique slug identifying the region.
        + name (string) - Descriptive name of the region.
    + Body

            {
                "regions": [
                    {
                        "id": 5,
                        "slug": "usw2",
                        "name": "US West"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": 6,
                        "slug": "use1",
                        "name": "US East"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": 7,
                        "slug": "euw1",
                        "name": "Europe"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": 8,
                        "slug": "apse1",
                        "name": "Asia"
                    }
                ]
            }


## Group Plans

A plan is a subscription plan. Each workstation requires a subscription to a
plan, with the exception of org plans.

### List All Plans [GET /plans/all]

+ Response 200 (application/json)
    + Attributes
        + id (number) - ID of plan.
        + slug (string) - A unique slug identifying the plan.
        + name (string) - Descriptive name of the plan.
        + price (number) - Monthly price of the plan, expressed in US cents.
        + cpu (string) - Number of CPUs a workstation under this plan will have.
        + memory (string) - Amount of RAM a workstation under this plan will have.
        + storage (string) - Amount of disk storage a workstation under this plan will have.
        + auto_shutdown (boolean) - If this is true, the workstation will automatically shutdown after a period of inactivity.
        + max_containers (number) - Maximum number of projects allowed on this workstation.
        + org (boolean) - True if this is an "org plan" (i.e. this is false for personal plans). Organization workstations can only be created under an org plan.
    + Body

            {
                "plans": [
                    {
                        "id": 47,
                        "slug": "free",
                        "name": "Free",
                        "description": "Optimal environment for learning and experimenting",
                        "price": 0,
                        "cpu": "1",
                        "memory": "1 GB",
                        "storage": "5 GB",
                        "auto_shutdown": true,
                        "max_containers": 1,
                        "org": false
                    },
                    {
                        "id": 41,
                        "slug": "20151125-basic",
                        "name": "Basic",
                        "description": "Ideal for developing small-scale projects and hobby apps",
                        "price": 1900,
                        "base_price": 0,
                        "hourly_price": 2828,
                        "cpu": "1",
                        "memory": "1 GB",
                        "storage": "10 GB",
                        "auto_shutdown": false,
                        "max_containers": 2,
                        "org": false
                    },
                    {
                        "id": 21,
                        "slug": "org-plus",
                        "name": "Org Plus",
                        "description": "",
                        "price": 11900,
                        "base_price": 0,
                        "hourly_price": 17709,
                        "cpu": "2",
                        "memory": "4 GB",
                        "storage": "80 GB",
                        "auto_shutdown": false,
                        "max_containers": 20,
                        "org": true
                    }
                ]
            }


## Group Templates

A template is a pre-defined development environment with tools pre-installed.
For example, the Ruby on Rails template comes with Rails and [rbenv](https://github.com/rbenv/rbenv)
already installed.

When creating a project, you'll probably want to create one using a template.

### List All Templates [GET /templates]

+ Response 200 (application/json)
